### Extended Raw Socket Capabilities

Packet injection and low-level packet capture rely on the `AF_PACKET` socket family (`net/packet/af_packet.c`), bypassing the IP and TCP/UDP protocol stacks.

#### Packet Socket Modes

* `SOCK_RAW`: Passes packet data including the link-layer (Ethernet/802.11) header directly to user space. The application crafts the complete frame, including source and destination MAC addresses.
* `SOCK_DGRAM`: Link-layer headers are managed by the kernel; user space receives or transmits data starting at the network layer protocol header (e.g., IP, ARP).

#### Zero-Copy Operations via `TPACKET_V3` Ring Buffers

To capture and transmit high volumes of traffic without system call overhead, applications use `TPACKET_V3` ring buffers (`packet_mmap`):

```c
/*
 * TPACKET_V3 Ring Buffer Initialization Example
 * Demonstrates high-performance raw frame capture interface setup.
 */
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <arpa/inet.h>
#include <linux/if_packet.h>
#include <net/ethernet.h>
#include <net/if.h>

int initialize_tpacket_v3_ring(int sock_fd) {
    struct tpacket_req3 req;
    memset(&req, 0, sizeof(req));

    /* Configure packet ring block sizes and frame counts */
    req.tp_block_size = 1024 * 1024; /* 1 MB Block Size */
    req.tp_block_nr = 8;             /* 8 Blocks total = 8 MB Ring */
    req.tp_frame_size = 2048;        /* Maximum frame size */
    req.tp_frame_nr = (req.tp_block_size * req.tp_block_nr) / req.tp_frame_size;
    req.tp_retire_blk_tov = 60;      /* Block retire timeout in ms */
    req.tp_feature_req_word = TP_FT_REQ_FILL_RXHASH;

    /* Set socket option to allocate kernel memory ring */
    if (setsockopt(sock_fd, SOL_PACKET, PACKET_RX_RING, &req, sizeof(req)) < 0) {
        perror("setsockopt: PACKET_RX_RING");
        return -1;
    }

    /* Map kernel memory buffer directly into user space process memory */
    uint8_t *ring_buffer = mmap(NULL, req.tp_block_size * req.tp_block_nr,
                                PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
                                MAP_SHARED | MAP_LOCKED, sock_fd, 0);

    if (ring_buffer == MAP_FAILED) {
        perror("mmap");
        return -1;
    }

    return 0;
}

```

#### Kernel Capabilities for Raw Network Access

Rather than granting full root execution privileges, execution can be restricted using fine-grained Linux Capabilities (`include/uapi/linux/capability.h`):

* `CAP_NET_RAW`: Permits creation of `AF_PACKET` and `AF_INET` raw sockets, as well as binding to arbitrary interface addresses.
* `CAP_NET_ADMIN`: Permits interface state alterations, promiscuous mode toggle (`IFF_PROMISC`), network routing table modifications, and channel changes on wireless devices.
* `CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE`: Permits socket binding to privileged port numbers (ports < 1024).

#### Advanced Socket Filtering & Device Binding

* `SO_ATTACH_FILTER`: Attaches a compiled Berkeley Packet Filter (BPF) program to the socket at the kernel level. Unmatched packets are discarded before reaching user-space ring buffers, reducing CPU consumption.
* `SO_BINDTODEVICE`: Binds the raw socket to a specific network device name (e.g., `eth0`, `wlan0mon`), preventing the socket from processing traffic originating from other interfaces.

