## 3. SECURITY POSTURE & PRIVILEGE MODEL

### Legacy vs. Modern Execution Models

Historically (pre-2020.1), Kali deployed a root-by-default execution paradigm. The default system account was `root` (UID 0). Modern releases enforce an unprivileged execution baseline using the standard `kali` account (UID 1000).

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                            PROCESS EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT                          |
|                                                                                   |
|  [ Non-Root User Context: UID 1000 (kali) ]                                       |
|      │                                                                            |
|      ├─► Direct Execution (Standard Binaries: wireshark, gdb)                      |
|      │                                                                            |
|      ├─► Elevation via Granular Sudo / Polkit                                     |
|      │       │                                                                    |
|      │       ▼                                                                    |
|      │   [ Transient UID 0 Context ] ──► System Modifications / Raw Network Operations|
|      │                                                                            |
|      └─► Capability Bounded Binaries (setcap)                                     |
|              │                                                                    |
|              ▼                                                                    |
|          [ CAP_NET_RAW / CAP_NET_ADMIN Only ] ──► Raw Socket / Packet Injection   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+

```

#### Attack Surface Reduction Mechanics

* **Prevention of GUI-Layer Exploitation:** Running browsers (`firefox-esr`), media parsers, or X11 clients as UID 0 exposes the entire system to complete compromise if a memory corruption or remote code execution (RCE) vulnerability is triggered.
* **Mitigation of Shared IPC/Socket Hijacking:** Restricting privileges prevents unprivileged processes from hijacking root-owned UNIX domain sockets, shared memory segments (`shmget`), or dynamic X11 session displays.
* **Strict Application Sandboxing:** Unprivileged users cannot write directly to system binaries (`/usr/bin`), shared libraries (`/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu`), or core kernel tunables (`/proc/sys/`).

---

### Sudo Scoping, Capabilities & Access Control

#### Granular Sudo Rule Design (`/etc/sudoers.d/kali-grant-capabilities`)

Instead of granting unrestricted root command access (`ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL`), sudo rules should restrict operational boundaries:

```ini
# Grant specific network analysis tools execution rights without password input
kali ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/nmap, /usr/bin/tcpdump, /usr/bin/dumpcap

# Prevent binary escape routes (e.g., shell spawning via nmap interactive flags or scripts)
# Command restrictions enforce specific binary path execution without global wildcard permissions

```

#### PolicyKit (Polkit) Graphical Authentication

GUI applications requiring administrative rights use Polkit to request elevated permissions without invoking direct SUID binaries or executing full X11 environments as root.

```javascript
// /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-kali-security-tools.rules
// Allow users in the 'sudo' group to execute specific administrative security tools without re-authentication
polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
    if ((action.id == "org.kali.pkexec.wireshark" ||
         action.id == "org.kali.pkexec.gparted") &&
        subject.isInGroup("sudo")) {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
});

```

#### Fine-Grained Capability Assignment (`setcap`)

To minimize binary exposure, specific POSIX capabilities are assigned directly to executables, eliminating the need for SUID permissions (`chmod +s`):

```bash
# Set CAP_NET_RAW and CAP_NET_ADMIN capabilities on custom sniffer binary
sudo setcap 'cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin=+eip' /usr/local/bin/custom_sniffer

# Inspect extended file attributes to verify capability assignment
getcap /usr/local/bin/custom_sniffer
# Output: /usr/local/bin/custom_sniffer = cap_net_admin,cap_net_raw+eip

```

