### Service Privilege Isolation & Sandboxing

#### Hardened systemd Unit Architecture

systemd service units for exposed network daemons, listeners, or payloads should enforce privilege containment to prevent lateral movement if a service is compromised.

```ini
# /etc/systemd/system/kali-network-listener.service
[Unit]
Description=Hardened Security Payload Listener Daemon
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/listener_daemon --port 4444
User=nobody
Group=nogroup

# Privilege Escalation & Capability Controls
NoNewPrivileges=true
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE CAP_NET_RAW
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE CAP_NET_RAW

# File System Isolation
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
ReadWritePaths=/var/log/listener_daemon/

# Network & System Call Filtering
ProtectClock=yes
RestrictNamespaces=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
SystemCallArchitectures=native
SystemCallFilter=@system-service ~@privileged @resources

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

```

#### Hardening Directive Reference

* `ProtectSystem=strict`: Mounts the entire file system hierarchy (`/usr`, `/boot`, `/etc`) read-only for the service process, except for explicit locations listed in `ReadWritePaths=`.
* `ProtectHome=yes`: Makes `/home`, `/root`, and `/run/user` invisible and inaccessible to the process, returning an empty directory.
* `NoNewPrivileges=true`: Ensures that the process and its children can never gain new privileges via `execve()` (e.g., SUID/SGID bit execution or file capabilities are ignored).
* `CapabilityBoundingSet=`: Drops all POSIX capabilities except those explicitly defined, preventing privileged kernel interactions even if the application switches context.
* `PrivateTmp=yes`: Mounts an isolated, process-specific `/tmp` and `/var/tmp` namespace, preventing file access or race-condition attacks against host temp files.
* `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes`: Denies memory mappings that are both writable and executable (`PROT_WRITE | PROT_EXEC`), neutralizing common shellcode buffer overflow vectors.

#### AppArmor Mandatory Access Control (MAC)

AppArmor enforces path-based access control rules for network tools and services.

```apparmor
# /etc/apparmor.d/usr.local.bin.listener_daemon
#include <tunables/global>

/usr/local/bin/listener_daemon {
  #include <abstractions/base>
  #include <abstractions/nameservice>

  # Network domain restrictions
  network inet stream,
  network inet6 stream,
  network raw,

  # File system path execution and read/write constraints
  /usr/local/bin/listener_daemon r,
  /var/log/listener_daemon/** rw,
  /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt r,

  # Explicit execution restrictions
  deny /bin/** x,
  deny /usr/bin/** x,
  deny /sbin/** x,
}

```

